<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<EncodingOptions xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <!--
    Seeded by boot2deb's `jellyfin-rockchip` feature. Jellyfin rewrites this file
    on every start, so these are starting values, not enforced ones: change them
    here for the next image, or in the Jellyfin dashboard for this one.

    Only the fields that differ from Jellyfin's own defaults appear. Anything
    absent keeps the default, including EncoderPreset, the CRF values, throttling,
    and AllowHevcEncoding (off — HEVC output is a per-site decision about what the
    clients can play, not a property of the hardware).
  -->

  <!--
    The absolute prefix path, not the /usr/bin/ffmpeg-rk symlink. Jellyfin takes no
    ffprobe path; it derives one by replacing the last component of this one. This
    path yields /opt/ffmpeg-rk/bin/ffprobe, which the ffmpeg-rk package ships. The
    symlink would yield /usr/bin/ffprobe, which this image does not install.
  -->
  <EncoderAppPath>/opt/ffmpeg-rk/bin/ffmpeg</EncoderAppPath>

  <!-- The only value that reaches h264_rkmpp / hevc_rkmpp on the VEPU580. -->
  <HardwareAccelerationType>rkmpp</HardwareAccelerationType>
  <EnableHardwareEncoding>true</EnableHardwareEncoding>

  <!--
    Empty because a stock jellyfin-server can do nothing useful with it here.

    The *_rkmpp decoders are compiled into ffmpeg-rk, so Jellyfin's capability
    probe (which reads `ffmpeg -hwaccels` and `-filters`) sees them and believes
    hardware decoding works. It does not: on a mainline kernel rkvdec is a V4L2
    stateless driver, MPP finds no decode client, and the rkmpp hwaccel fails to
    open. FFmpeg then decodes in software without saying so — measured on the RK1,
    a 1080p HEVC transcode costs 50.4 CPU-seconds with `-hwaccel rkmpp` against
    50.2 with no hwaccel at all. So listing codecs here is inert rather than
    harmful; it buys nothing and hides a software decode behind a hardware label.

    Hardware decode does reach FFmpeg here, via `-hwaccel v4l2request`, and it is
    the largest single saving available on this board: the same transcode costs
    17.0 CPU-seconds. Jellyfin cannot emit that flag on its own, because
    HardwareAccelerationType is a closed enum with no v4l2request member.
    `patches/jellyfin/0001` makes the rkmpp type emit it when the FFmpeg build
    carries the hwaccel. With a server carrying that patch, fill this list with
    the codecs rkvdec decodes on this SoC — h264, hevc, vp9 — and 10-bit content
    keeps decoding in software on its own, because v4l2request cannot transfer
    NV15 to system memory.
  -->
  <HardwareDecodingCodecs />

  <!--
    ffmpeg-rk is built without OpenCL, so there is no hardware tone-map to reach
    and enabling either of these would only cost CPU on a software path.
  -->
  <EnableTonemapping>false</EnableTonemapping>
  <EnableVppTonemapping>false</EnableVppTonemapping>
</EncodingOptions>
